[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Council-backed Sutton Living Ltd seeking \u00a30.5m additional funding due to cost overruns on Grove redevelopment scheme.","lead":"Sutton has a shareholding in SLL and is exposed to overrun risk on this commercial development. If the scheme does not generate sufficient sale proceeds, the council's loan exposure increases and repayment timescale extends.","iso":"2026-07-13","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Education Health Care Plans (EHCPs) have grown by 250 cases in 24 months (2,469 to 2,719), driving ongoing SEND Block overspend despite transformation gains.","lead":"EHCP growth is a structural, demographic driver of High Needs Block spending. A lender should note this is not discretionary spend and the transformation has only slowed the rate of budget deterioration, not stopped it.","iso":"2026-07-13","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Government's CIL relief package will cost Sutton up to \u00a312.9m in foregone borough-level community infrastructure funding over past 10 years.","lead":"A lender needs to understand that Sutton faces a hidden cost from national policy\u2014infrastructure funding will be deferred or cut, which will impact either future capital programmes or put pressure on the revenue budget to backfill.","iso":"2026-07-13","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-13","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","headline":"Council-backed Sutton Living Ltd seeking \u00a30.5m additional funding due to cost overruns on Grove redevelopment scheme.","detail":"SLL has requested an increase to the Grove loan facility from \u00a34.9m to \u00a35.4m due to 'increase in the build and professional costs to deliver their scheme'. The council notes the scheme 'is expected to deliver a high-quality residential conversion which preserves and enhances an important local heritage asset', but the cost pressures indicate construction challenges.","category":"capital","matters":"Sutton has a shareholding in SLL and is exposed to overrun risk on this commercial development. If the scheme does not generate sufficient sale proceeds, the council's loan exposure increases and repayment timescale extends.","figures":"\u00a30.5m facility increase, total facility now \u00a35.4m","quote":"This increase has been requested by SLL due to an increase in the build and professional costs to deliver their scheme","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-13","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","headline":"Education Health Care Plans (EHCPs) have grown by 250 cases in 24 months (2,469 to 2,719), driving ongoing SEND Block overspend despite transformation gains.","detail":"Although the SEND transformation programme has reduced independent and out-of-borough special school variance from \u00a36.60m (Q1) to \u00a34.88m, the underlying demand is growing. EHCPs have increased from 2,469 in April 2024 to 2,719 in April 2026. The government has announced full SEND funding from 2028/29, but this does not cover the transition period and councils must absorb growth until then.","category":"demand","matters":"EHCP growth is a structural, demographic driver of High Needs Block spending. A lender should note this is not discretionary spend and the transformation has only slowed the rate of budget deterioration, not stopped it.","figures":"EHCPs increased from 2,469 (April 2024) to 2,719 (April 2026), 250 case increase","quote":"The number of EHCPs has increased from 2,469 in April 2024 to 2,719 in April 2026","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-13","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","headline":"Government's CIL relief package will cost Sutton up to \u00a312.9m in foregone borough-level community infrastructure funding over past 10 years.","detail":"The council strongly opposed the government's temporary CIL relief to stimulate housebuilding, warning that 'the proposed relief is highly unlikely to meaningfully stimulate development while conversely triggering significant revenue deficits for essential infrastructure'. The council has calculated that up to \u00a312.9m of borough-level CIL could be foregone, of which \u00a38.9m relates to current pipeline schemes. This funding is the 'primary mechanism for funding strategic infrastructure' including transport, healthcare and schools.","category":"income","matters":"A lender needs to understand that Sutton faces a hidden cost from national policy\u2014infrastructure funding will be deferred or cut, which will impact either future capital programmes or put pressure on the revenue budget to backfill.","figures":"Up to \u00a312.9m foregone CIL receipts, \u00a38.9m in current pipeline","quote":"As CIL is the primary mechanism for funding the strategic infrastructure necessitated by growth - including transport improvements, healthcare facilities, school expansions, and public open spaces - this potential shortfall creates a critical funding risk","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-13","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","headline":"HRA damp and mould remediation overran budget by approximately \u00a31m and is expected to continue identifying additional works.","detail":"In 2025/26, approximately 666 damp and mould cases were recorded across 6,000 homes (11% of stock). Capital spend overran the \u00a32.9m budget significantly, with the paper noting 'additional revenue costs linked to inspections, repairs, disrepair and specialist resources'. The ongoing stock condition survey is only 72% complete and is expected to identify more issues in the short term, 'increasing short-term demand and cost'.","category":"demand","matters":"A lender should note Sutton faces regulatory compliance risk (Awaab's Law, HHSRS) that is driving unbudgeted capital and revenue spend. The 11% of stock affected suggests this is a major portfolio issue, not edge case.","figures":"666 damp and mould cases, approximately \u00a31m overspend against \u00a32.9m budget, 11% of 6,000 homes affected","quote":"damp and mould contributed to significant financial pressure, including c.\u00a31m overspend against the approved \u00a32.9m capital budget, alongside additional revenue costs linked to inspections, repairs, disrepair and specialist resources","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-13","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Performance and Outturn 2025/26 - Report, 22/06/2026 Strategy and Resources Committee , item 6d","paper_url":"https://sutton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s96054/Financial Performance and Outturn 202526 - Report 22062026 Strategy and Resources Committee.pdf","headline":"2026/27 budget carries \u00a31.20m of unmitigated risk from savings under-delivery and growth shortfall.","detail":"The initial review of 2026/27 deliverability found \u00a30.62m of savings will not be delivered (\u00a312.70m vs \u00a313.32m budgeted) and \u00a30.58m of planned growth is insufficient (actual need is \u00a313.50m vs \u00a312.92m budgeted). The committee statement that this 'is expected to be mitigated by other savings activity through the year' is forward-looking and contingent on performance.","category":"savings","matters":"Sutton is entering 2026/27 with known shortfalls equivalent to 0.5% of the total services budget. This signals either weak savings planning or continued demand pressures not yet priced into growth.","figures":"\u00a30.62m savings under-delivery, \u00a30.58m growth shortfall, total \u00a31.20m","quote":"The initial review has confirmed that \u00a312.70m of savings will be delivered, indicating an under-delivery of \u00a30.62m. In respect of budgeted growth, \u00a312.92m was provided to services but the initial review forecasts that this will be insufficient by \u00a30.58m","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-13"},"name":"Sutton","findings_total":77,"news_drafts_total":3}}